s in the polls do you think  that this was always inevitable and  there's an understanding that it was or  are things going especially bad at the  moment  i think he was always going to become  more unpopular over time particularly  because of you as you say the trajectory  of spending lots of money and paying  people's salaries um to suddenly saying  we're raising taxes and it's gonna be  tough cost of living and we can't do  everything to help you that is tricky i  think what is making it a bit worse  right now though is obviously i think  just personal uh disclosures stories  about his wife's potential tax affairs  and i think that's clearly a subject  that makes rishi like very uncomfortable  he um gave an interview and recently  saying you know it's not fair game to  come after my wife and i think what  we're seeing now is how does he handle  this level of scrutiny and pressure i  think that in some interviews he has  seemed um quite irritated by it and  therefore i think  what will help rishi sunaka's full  transparency and a calm approach and  what will make it worse is if  if it starts to look bad tempered and i  think when it comes to tax affairs you  can obviously say your wife's a private  individual that's what we hear from the  treasury and so she shouldn't expect  help to the same standards as a  politician i think most people would  accept that but i think there's still  some legitimate questions that should be  answered  katie thanks for joining me  and finally paul wood writes in this  week's magazine about the kolonewski  battalion a small group of volunteers  from belarus who are fighting for  ukraine against russia what does this  say about the position of belarusian  dictator alexander lukashenko could a  larger resistance from inside the  country threaten his regime paul joins  me now  paul thanks for joining spectator tv in  your piece for the magazine this week  you talk about the small group of  belarusian volunteer soldiers who are on  the 