se  acts of sabotage be enough to undermine  the russian war machine  and do you think that it's this small  battalion in particular that's putting  the pressure on putin's relationship  with lukashenko  one of a number of things i think  far more significant  would be the attitude of the belarusian  army now a month ago there were a lot of  confident predictions that president  lukashenko would order the belarusian  army into action alongside russian  forces there are only 45 000 of them and  i spoke to one belarusian journalist you  thought  the actual number of combat troops they  could field would be only 10 000 not  enough to make a huge difference on the  battlefield but helpful enough to  vladimir putin's war effort but that  never happened and i think it didn't  happen um because of the fears that  president lukashenko might reasonably  have that the army would refuse to carry  out orders either it would go through a  show of crossing the border and carrying  out some what would describe to me as  fake attacks while staying as far away  from the ukrainian forces as possible  all they could outright mutilate and  certainly those are the hopes of the  belarusian opposition if orders were  given by president lukashenko cross the  border south into ukraine that the army  would mutilate so the idea that these  belarusian volunteers  two three four hundred of them however  they are would turn around and march  back to mints to overthrow president  lukashenko i think that is fanciful  but a far bigger risk and a far more  realistic risk for him is that he is  pushed into blackmailed into joining the  war by president putin his own army  mutinies and that is the signal for a  more generalized uprising against him  if lukashenko doesn't order his army to  go and help the russians in ukraine is  he safe from mutiny or is there still a  chance that his own army might rebel  against him or that they might decide to  go and join the small battalion and grow  it in size  